  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Deletion controls are underspecified: define the deletion count, minimum history length, common eligible rows, and whether older/random deletion preserves the final event. Otherwise recent-event effects conflate final-item removal with broader recency sensitivity.",
    "Repacking changes event positions and potentially HSTU relative-position or temporal biases. The report needs controls separating removed information from position changes, plus clarification of whether the supplied ModelView uses timestamps.",
    "Reproducibility requires explicit margin-change direction, tie handling, zero-norm cosine handling, intervention aggregation, and RNG isolation so diagnostics do not change subsequent training or sampling.",
    "Training-pair sensitivity cannot establish recommendation usefulness or expected HR@10 gains. The future-potential score of 8 is speculative; implementation difficulty of 3 depends on verified ModelView scoring semantics and positional controls."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Find controls that distinguish final-item dependence, broader ordering, and positional artifacts before interpreting temporal sensitivity.",
      "query": "For HSTU sequential recommendation (Zhai et al., 2024, arXiv:2402.17152), how do relative positional and temporal attention biases interact with left-padded history deletion and repacking? What controlled deletion and final-item-preserving shuffle designs separate information removal from positional changes and distribution shift?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess whether this diagnostic should guide model changes and calibrate its claimed potential against alternatives.",
      "query": "What empirical studies validate whether inference-time history deletion or shuffling sensitivity in sequential recommenders predicts held-out ranking improvements? Compare training positive-negative margin changes with fixed-candidate target-rank changes, and identify limitations from sampled negatives, repeated user histories, and diagnostic-driven validation overfitting."
    }
  ]
}
